# Global against local tone mapping, side by side.
#
# The global operator applies ONE curve to every pixel: no halos, trivially
# fast, and structurally unable to give a bright region more of the display
# without taking the same from everywhere else. On a merged bracket the sky
# keeps its ordering and still reads flat.
#
# The local operator splits the image into ILLUMINATION (large scale, hugely
# variable) and REFLECTANCE (small scale, bounded by what materials do),
# compresses only the illumination, and puts the detail back untouched. The
# split is in log space, which is what makes local contrast survive the
# compression identically in bright and dark regions alike.
#
# params() gives each operator its own group of sliders, so both are tunable
# in one panel. Watch for halos around strong edges -- a bright rim on the
# dark side of a skyline is the failure mode, and the reason global stays
# the default.

src = image("test")

src => params(tonemap)()       => display("1 global")
src => params(tonemap_local)() => display("2 local")
